Done. Added `rename` to `rust/src/store.rs:20-40`.

```rust
pub fn rename(dir: &Path, old_id: &str, new_id: &str, new_title: &str) -> std::io::Result<()> {
    fs::create_dir_all(dir)?;
    let old_path = dir.join(format!("{}.note", old_id));
    let content = fs::read_to_string(&old_path)?;
    let parts: Vec<&str> = content.splitn(4, SEP).collect();
    if parts.len() != 4 {
        return Err(std::io::Error::new(
            std::io::ErrorKind::InvalidData,
            "stored note has unexpected format",
        ));
    }
    let new_content = format!(
        "{}{}{}{}{}{}{}",
        new_id, SEP, new_title, SEP, parts[2], SEP, parts[3]
    );
    fs::write(dir.join(format!("{}.note", new_id)), new_content)?;
    if old_id != new_id {
        fs::remove_file(&old_path)?;
    }
    Ok(())
}
```

How it works: reads the old `{old_id}.note`, re-serializes it with the new id and title while reusing the existing pinned flag (`parts[2]`) and body (`parts[3]`), writes the new file, then deletes the old one. The `old_id != new_id` guard avoids deleting a file you just overwrote when renaming onto the same id.

Verified: `cargo test` passes (existing + a temporary rename test confirming `load_all` returns exactly one note with the new id/title, unchanged body/tags/pinned, and no `old_id.note` left on disk). The temp test file was removed; the only change is the new function in `store.rs`.


